{"name":"Sleepy Kitten","hex":"#a2a1a1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sleepy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Tired; feeling the need for sleep.","Suggesting tiredness."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The gum that builds up in the eye; sleep, gound.","Shingleback."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sleepy"},{"term":"kitten","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A young cat, especially before sexual maturity (reached at about seven months).","A young rabbit, rat, hedgehog, squirrel, fox, beaver, badger, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To give birth to kittens."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kitten"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kitten","blocks":[{"text":"A kitten is a juvenile cat. After being born, kittens display primary altriciality and are fully dependent on their mothers for survival. They normally do not open their eyes for seven to ten days. After about two weeks, kittens develop quickly and begin to explore the world beyond their nest. After a further three to four weeks, they begin to eat solid food and grow baby teeth. Domestic kittens are highly social animals and usually enjoy human companionship."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Juvenile_Ragdoll.jpg/330px-Juvenile_Ragdoll.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kitten"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/1_dia_de_vida.jpg/500px-1_dia_de_vida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/1_dia_de_vida.jpg/1280px-1_dia_de_vida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/1_dia_de_vida.jpg/500px-1_dia_de_vida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt